  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\nSouthwest flow aloft will become more zonal by tomorrow. Short wave\nridging will develop into the region late in the week as the flow\nbuckles in response to a substantial short wave moving into the \nPlains.\n\nLow level flow will be southerly until Thursday night when a cold \nfront slides through the area in response to a short wave passing \nwell north of the area. Cannot completely rule out an isolated shower\nwith the front along and north of I-70. While the chance of \nmeasurable rain is non-zero, it is still extremely low and thus the \nforecast remains dry. High pressure will then build into the area \nFriday and move off to the east on Saturday. \n\nAbove normal temperatures will continue, although in the wake of the\nfront, readings will be not quite as warm. Highs may only be 7 to 10\ndegrees above normal on Saturday compared to 10 to 15 degrees above\nnormal today and Thursday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\nMuch of the guidance suite has been trending slightly slower and a \nbit further south with the track of a short wave that will move \nthrough the region Sunday into Sunday night.(GEFS is the slowest in \nthis trend.) Still expecting deeper moisture to accompany this, but \nforcing may not be very impressive. Even so, this should be the end \nof the extended dry period, but rainfall may end up not being very \nmuch, especially across northern counties. We shall see how guidance\nsolutions evolve through the rest of the week. \n\nThis will be the beginning of a more active pattern next week.\nEnsemble spread increases, as is usually the case, which smears the\nPoP forecast through Tuesday. It will not be wet all of the time, but\nthe chance of rain will be present through the latter part of the \nforecast.",
